meta { title = "Console I/O and formatting", description = "Read and write Unicode text with std/io and format scalar values with std/fmt.", order = 5, group = "Library reference" }

Import console functions from std/io and formatting from std/fmt. Standard modules are part of the compiler and need no package dependency or network access. They are ordinary explicit imports, rather than names added to every source module.

h2 { "print" }

code { "print(text: String) -> unit ! IoError" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import print, println, IoError

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  try print("Hello, ")
  try println("Kalandra!")
  return ()
""" }

print(text: String) -> unit ! IoError writes text to standard output. println has the same signature and adds one newline. Neither inserts spaces, quotes or escape notation. Embedded zero bytes are preserved in redirected streams.

A console call serializes its entire write, including the newline, against other calls using the same stream. Several calls can still interleave with calls from other tasks: combine text into one call when it must stay together. Writes are observable effects and cannot be undone if a later operation fails.

h2 { "println" }

code { "println(text: String) -> unit ! IoError" }

Write text and one newline to standard output. The write and newline form one console operation.

h2 { "eprint" }

code { "eprint(text: String) -> unit ! IoError" }

eprint(text: String) -> unit ! IoError and eprintln(text: String) -> unit ! IoError write to standard error. eprintln adds one newline. Use them for diagnostics so users can redirect standard output independently. Ordering between the two streams is not guaranteed.

h2 { "eprintln" }

code { "eprintln(text: String) -> unit ! IoError" }

Write text and one newline to standard error.

h2 { "readLine" }

code { "readLine(maxBytes: u64) -> Line ! IoError" }

readLine(maxBytes: u64) -> Line ! IoError reads one line from standard input. The limit is required and counts UTF-8 bytes in the returned text, excluding the line ending.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import readLine, println, Line, IoError

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  line = try readLine(4096)
  try match line:
    Line.End: println("No input.")
    Line.Text {text}: println(text)
  return ()
""" }

Line is an enum with two variants: Line.End means end of input, and Line.Text holds a line in its text: String field. An empty line is Text with an empty String, not End. The function removes LF and CRLF endings. A final line without a newline is returned before End. Text must be valid UTF-8; no normalization is applied.

A line exceeding maxBytes is consumed through its ending and produces IoError.LineTooLong. The next read begins at the next line. A limit of zero accepts an empty line and rejects nonempty text. readLine does not allocate an array containing the entire input stream.

h2 { "IoError" }

Console functions declare IoError, with these variants:

list {
  "IoError.System {code: i32, message: String}: an operating-system I/O error. code is platform-specific; message describes it.",
  "IoError.InvalidEncoding: input is not valid Unicode text.",
  "IoError.LineTooLong {limit: u64}: a line exceeds its requested byte limit."
}

Propagate errors with try, or recover with catch and match. Allocation faults and cancellation retain their normal runtime behavior; they are not converted into IoError.System.

h2 { "format" }

code { "format(template: String, values...) -> String" }

format(template, values...) -> String is provided by std/fmt. The template must be a String literal. An empty brace pair consumes one value. Double an opening or closing brace to include it literally. The compiler checks placeholder count and argument types.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/fmt" import format
from "std/io" import println, IoError

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  try println(format("count={}, ready={}, name={}", 42, true, "Kalandra"))
  try println(format("{{{}}}", 42))
  return ()
""" }

The output is count=42, ready=true, name=Kalandra followed by 42 enclosed in braces on the next line. Arguments are evaluated once, from left to right. The result is an owned String.

Supported values are String, bool and the ten numeric scalar types: i8, i16, i32, i64, u8, u16, u32, u64, f32 and f64. Integers use decimal notation; booleans use true and false. Floating-point formatting preserves enough information to round-trip at the value's source precision. Formatting is independent of the process locale.

Arrays, tensors, records, unit, alignment and precision specifiers are not accepted. Format individual elements or choose the fields you want to display. A dynamically computed template is not accepted, and format cannot be stored or passed as a first-class function.

h2 { "Tasks and devices" }

Console operations run on the CPU. Within structured tasks, a pending read or write suspends its task so another ready task can run, including when the program uses a single worker. Cancellation can stop waiting tasks and settle their owned values; an operating-system operation that has already started can finish independently. Cancellation cannot retract bytes already written.

Use direct host calls for console I/O in programs compiled for GPU targets. Passing an I/O function as a callback is supported by CPU-targeted programs only. Console operations and Strings cannot be used inside device kernels.

Ordinary sequential applications use direct host I/O when the compiler proves that scheduling is unnecessary. Explicit parallel work keeps task admission checks, cancellation and task resource limits. The explicit readLine bound still applies in both cases.

